// payment.compact — one deploy, global, and the only contract here that can never be replaced.
//
// `handle.compact`'s `bind` accepts a permit of colour `tokenType(permitDomain(tier), payment)`, where
// `payment` is **this contract's address**, sealed into every handle template. So a successor mints
// colours the frozen template will not accept, and pre-deploying spare generations does not help —
// their code would have to be written today. Everything below is chosen with that in mind: what the
// *code* can do is frozen forever, so the code has to be the part that never needs to change.
//
// **The currency is state, not code.** `prices` is a map behind `setPrice`, so which currencies are
// accepted, and at what price, changes at any time without a redeploy. What cannot change is which
// receive primitive a purchase circuit calls — `receiveUnshielded` and `receiveShielded` are different
// primitives, and a contract carrying only one can never take a currency on the other however many
// `setPrice` calls follow. That is the reason both rails exist here rather than the one we expect to
// launch with (`docs/BOUNDED_STATE_ARCHITECTURE.md` §3.3).
//
// **The treasury forwards; it does not hold.** A contract that banks its takings publishes its float —
// OpenZeppelin's `ShieldedTreasury` documents exactly that, and "shielded" there names the token rail,
// not the balance. Forwarding in the same circuit removes both the public float and `sweep()`, at the
// cost of pinning the treasury at construction (§3.4.2). `bricktowers/midnight-identity`'s shop does
// the same in two lines, so the shape has prior art on chain rather than only in a design note.
//
// **Authority is a rotatable commitment, not a frozen one.** Midnight has no in-circuit signature
// check and Compact 0.23 does not expose the ledger's authenticated caller (`kernel.caller()` is
// undefined, and `ownPublicKey()` is prover-controlled — the docs warn against it explicitly). So the
// authority is a hash preimage, which is what OpenZeppelin's `Ownable` does too. The part that matters
// is that `adminAuth` is **mutable**: `rotateAdmin` moves it, so a leaked secret is recoverable. An
// earlier draft sealed it, which put it in the address and made a leak permanent for no benefit —
// `ZOwnablePK` seals the instance *salt* and leaves the authority mutable, and that is the right split.
//
// ⚠️ **An unshielded permit can be minted *to a third party* with nothing but their address; a
// shielded one cannot.** A shielded recipient here is a `ZswapCoinPublicKey` — half of a shielded
// address. The other half, the encryption key, is what a transaction builder needs to attach the
// ciphertext that lets the recipient find the coin by scanning, and it appears nowhere in the
// circuit. A builder holding the recipient's *full* shielded address can supply it and the coin is
// found normally; a builder holding only what this contract can express cannot, and the coin is
// stranded. That asymmetry is what forced "every mint names its caller" on the shielded design.
// Unshielded tokens are ledger UTxOs with nothing to scan for and no second key to exchange, so a
// grant to someone else is plainly deliverable. `grantPermit` therefore takes a recipient, and
// choosing the unshielded rail is what buys that back.
pragma language_version 0.23;

import CompactStandardLibrary;

/// Where an **unshielded** purchase is forwarded, in the same circuit that receives it. Sealed, so it
/// is part of this contract's address and cannot be repointed by anyone who later learns `adminAuth`.
export sealed ledger treasury: Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>;

/// Where a **shielded** purchase is forwarded.
///
/// ⚠️ A second field rather than a second use of the first, because the two rails address recipients
/// with **disjoint types**: `sendUnshielded` takes `Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>` and
/// `sendImmediateShielded` takes `Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>`. There is no one value
/// that names the treasury on both rails. Sealed and free — a ledger field costs no entry point.
export sealed ledger shieldedTreasury: Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>;

/// `persistentHash(["pnyx:admin", grantSecret])`. **Mutable** — `rotateAdmin` is the recovery path,
/// and the reason this is not `sealed`.
///
/// ⚠️ Rotation limits the *window*, not the damage: a leak still mints permits and reprices tiers until
/// we notice, and names claimed cheap in that window stay claimed. It also loses a race to an attacker
/// who rotates first. Neither is a reason to freeze it — a frozen secret loses that race permanently.
///
/// ⚠️ Its initial value comes from the constructor and so lands in this contract's address. That is why
/// the deploy script must generate it with a CSPRNG and refuse a low-entropy value: the preprod deploy
/// used `bytes32(0x5a)` and the preimage fell out of the public hash in 3.8 ms.
export ledger adminAuth: Bytes<32>;

/// `(tier, currency) -> price`. The whole of the currency policy, and the only growing structure in
/// this contract — bounded by tiers x currencies, which we choose, rather than by usage.
///
/// A purchase records **nothing**: permit double-spend is Zswap's and the ledger's problem, not ours,
/// so there is no nullifier set to accumulate and this contract does not grow with use.
export ledger prices: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>;

/// The preimage of `adminAuth`.
witness grantSecret(): Bytes<32>;

/// The preimage `rotateAdmin` installs. Supplied as a witness rather than a parameter so a rotation
/// never puts the incoming secret in a transaction — only its hash reaches the chain.
witness nextAdminSecret(): Bytes<32>;

/// One permit colour per **tier**, not per name and not one for everything. Per-name pricing would need
/// a map keyed by name — the growing structure this whole design exists to remove — and a single colour
/// forecloses tiered pricing outright (§3.4).
circuit permitDomain(tier: Uint<8>): Bytes<32> {
  return tier == 0 ? pad(32, "pnyx:permit:t0") :
         tier == 1 ? pad(32, "pnyx:permit:t1") :
         tier == 2 ? pad(32, "pnyx:permit:t2") :
         tier == 3 ? pad(32, "pnyx:permit:t3") :
         tier == 4 ? pad(32, "pnyx:permit:t4") :
         tier == 5 ? pad(32, "pnyx:permit:t5") : pad(32, "pnyx:permit:t6");
}

/// The per-name transfer capability `rebind` spends. Minted on request and never purchasable: pricing
/// it per name needs a per-name map, and making it purchasable reopens snipe-and-resell.
circuit rebindDomain(name: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<Vector<2, Bytes<32>>>([pad(32, "pnyx:rebind"), name]);
}

/// The per-**name** moderation capability that `handle.compact`'s `moderate` spends.
///
/// Per name rather than one token for the namespace, so a stolen one silences a single name instead of
/// all of them. And minted *here* rather than gated by a secret in the handle template, because there
/// is one handle contract per name: a per-handle authority could only be rotated one transaction per
/// name, whereas this makes the whole namespace's moderation authority rotate with `rotateAdmin` alone.
/// A frozen template can compute this colour; it could never read a mutable field out of this contract.
circuit moderateDomain(name: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<Vector<2, Bytes<32>>>([pad(32, "pnyx:moderate"), name]);
}

/// What `prices` is keyed by. Domain-separated so a colour can never collide with a tier domain, which
/// would let a currency's own colour name a price row.
circuit priceKey(tier: Uint<8>, color: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<Vector<3, Bytes<32>>>([pad(32, "pnyx:price"), permitDomain(tier), color]);
}

/// The colour a **free** tier's row is written under.
///
/// Free is a currency, which is what keeps the admin surface at zero new circuits: `setPrice` already
/// adds and removes rows, so `setPrice(tier, freeColor(), 1)` opens a free tier and
/// `setPrice(tier, freeColor(), 0)` closes it. ⚠️ The row's *amount* is never read — only its presence
/// — so the 1 is a placeholder, not a price.
///
/// It cannot collide with a real currency: a currency colour is a token type, and this is a domain hash
/// nobody can mint under.
circuit freeColor(): Bytes<32> {
  return pad(32, "pnyx:free");
}

circuit adminCommitment(secret: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<Vector<2, Bytes<32>>>([pad(32, "pnyx:admin"), secret]);
}

circuit requireAdmin(): [] {
  assert(adminCommitment(grantSecret()) == adminAuth, "not authorised");
}

constructor(
  pay: Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>,
  shieldedPay: Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>,
  admin: Bytes<32>,
) {
  treasury = disclose(pay);
  shieldedTreasury = disclose(shieldedPay);
  adminAuth = disclose(admin);
}

/// Hand admin authority to a new secret. The recovery path, and the whole reason `adminAuth` is not
/// sealed: without it a single leak would be permanent on the one contract that can never be replaced.
///
/// The incoming secret is a witness, so only its commitment is ever published — the same property
/// `Ownable.transferOwnership` gets by taking the new owner's *id* rather than their key.
export circuit rotateAdmin(): [] {
  requireAdmin();
  adminAuth = disclose(adminCommitment(nextAdminSecret()));
}

/// Add, remove or reprice any currency for any tier, at any time. A price of zero removes the row,
/// which is how a currency stops being accepted without the code knowing it ever was.
///
/// This is the circuit that makes the launch currency a decision we do not have to take today.
export circuit setPrice(tier: Uint<8>, color: Bytes<32>, price: Uint<128>): [] {
  requireAdmin();
  const key = priceKey(disclose(tier), disclose(color));
  // Disclosed up front: branching on it is itself a disclosure of the comparison's result, so the
  // compiler refuses the `if` otherwise. Nothing here is private — a price list is public by design.
  const amount = disclose(price);
  if (amount == 0) {
    assert(prices.member(key), "no such price");
    prices.remove(key);
  } else {
    prices.insert(key, amount);
  }
}

/// Buy a permit with an **unshielded** currency, and forward the payment in the same circuit.
///
/// The expected rail at launch: bridged USDM lands unshielded, and it is the only stablecoin on this
/// chain a contract can actually reach — ShieldUSD is a shielded *contract* token, and contract-to-
/// contract calls do not exist on our pinned toolchain. What it costs is privacy, not feasibility: an
/// unshielded payment puts payer, payee and amount in the clear.
export circuit buyPermitUnshielded(
  tier: Uint<8>,
  color: Bytes<32>,
  recipient: Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>,
): [] {
  const t = disclose(tier);
  const currency = disclose(color);
  const key = priceKey(t, currency);
  assert(prices.member(key), "currency not accepted for this tier");
  const price = prices.lookup(key);
  receiveUnshielded(currency, price);
  sendUnshielded(currency, price, treasury);
  mintUnshieldedToken(permitDomain(t), 1, disclose(recipient));
}

/// Buy a permit with a **shielded** currency, forwarding the coin in the same circuit.
///
/// Nothing we can accept today needs this. It exists because the choice of receive primitive is frozen
/// code where the choice of currency is mutable state — a purchase circuit with only the unshielded
/// rail could never take a shielded currency, on the one contract that can never be redeployed. The
/// coin arrives in this transaction and so has no Merkle index, which is why the forward is
/// `sendImmediateShielded` rather than `sendShielded`.
export circuit buyPermitShielded(
  tier: Uint<8>,
  coin: ShieldedCoinInfo,
  recipient: Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>,
): [] {
  const paid = disclose(coin);
  const t = disclose(tier);
  const key = priceKey(t, paid.color);
  assert(prices.member(key), "currency not accepted for this tier");
  assert(paid.value >= prices.lookup(key), "payment below price");
  receiveShielded(paid);
  sendImmediateShielded(paid, shieldedTreasury, paid.value);
  mintUnshieldedToken(permitDomain(t), 1, disclose(recipient));
}

/// Mint a claim permit for free, to anyone. Reserved names, airdrops and support cases.
///
/// ⚠️ Takes a recipient, and that is only sound because the permit is **unshielded**. A shielded grant
/// would need the recipient's encryption key — the half of a shielded address this contract's
/// `ZswapCoinPublicKey` cannot carry — exchanged out of band before the mint, or the coin is stranded.
/// That is why the shielded design had to mint to the caller and hand the grant over as an ordinary
/// transfer.
export circuit grantPermit(tier: Uint<8>, recipient: Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>): [] {
  requireAdmin();
  mintUnshieldedToken(permitDomain(disclose(tier)), 1, disclose(recipient));
}

/// Mint a permit for a tier the admin has opened to everyone, for nothing.
///
/// **The point is a wallet with nothing in it.** A price — even a nominal one — cannot be paid by someone
/// who has just installed the app, and that is exactly who needs a name. `grantPermit` mints for nothing
/// too, but only for whoever holds `adminAuth`: a faucet service, and a secret on a server that mints
/// permits and reprices every tier if it leaks. This needs no secret at all.
///
/// ⚠️ **Deliberately unbounded, and it records nothing.** No nullifier, no claimant set, no per-caller
/// state: anyone may call this as often as they like while the row is open. Two reasons, and the second
/// is the one that decided it:
///
/// 1. A free tier is a **long-name** tier by construction — t6 is 23 to 31 bytes, which is more names
///    than anyone can exhaust — so squatting it costs the squatter and denies nobody.
/// 2. Bounding it means remembering who claimed, and this is the one contract that **must not grow with
///    use**: it is deployed once and can never be replaced, so anything accumulating here accumulates
///    for the life of the namespace. A once-per-identity set was written and then deleted for exactly
///    that reason — it also bought no Sybil resistance, since an identity is a seed.
///
/// So the admin's lever is the row: `setPrice(tier, freeColor(), 1)` opens a giveaway and a price of
/// zero ends it. Which tiers are free is a decision, revisited whenever, and never a property of code.
export circuit claimFreePermit(tier: Uint<8>, recipient: Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>): [] {
  const t = disclose(tier);
  // Presence, not amount: a free row says "this tier is free", and `setPrice` is what writes it.
  assert(prices.member(priceKey(t, freeColor())), "this tier is not free");
  mintUnshieldedToken(permitDomain(t), 1, disclose(recipient));
}

/// Mint the per-name transfer capability `handle.compact`'s `rebind` spends.
export circuit grantRebindPermit(name: Bytes<32>, recipient: Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>): [] {
  requireAdmin();
  mintUnshieldedToken(rebindDomain(disclose(name)), 1, disclose(recipient));
}

/// Mint the per-name moderation capability `handle.compact`'s `moderate` spends. Reusable — `moderate`
/// takes it back — so one mint serves that name for ever and a ban is not rationed by a mint.
///
/// ⚠️ The only authority here whose theft is **reversible**: `moderate` writes `banned` and has no
/// `owner` parameter, so a stolen mod token can silence a name and never take one.
export circuit grantModeratePermit(
  name: Bytes<32>,
  recipient: Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>,
): [] {
  requireAdmin();
  mintUnshieldedToken(moderateDomain(disclose(name)), 1, disclose(recipient));
}
